From d569372feac245a25ff7b991b59e9c10f02a880d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mike Kinney <mike.kinney@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 13:25:14 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] fix(import): refuse a STEP face whose tessellation never
 converges

truck's surface subdivision recurses forever on a B-spline or NURBS face
that is not continuous (one seam stays "far" at every depth), inside a
rayon task nothing can interrupt, and with no facet produced the facet
budget never fires: the import hung (#1536, a 62-face filleted cube ran
for 19 CPU-minutes).

Every face is now wrapped in a metered surface (step_guard). B-spline and
NURBS faces are divided by truck's own generic parameter_division called
on the wrapper - the same algorithm, so the same mesh - and past
FACE_EVAL_CAP evaluations in one pass the wrapper answers with a fixed
point (the subdivision stops within one sweep) and an empty division (no
runaway grid is triangulated). The import is then refused naming the
face's STEP entity, index, kind and shell. No thread is spawned, nothing
outlives the call.

Pinned by import_step_guard (a discontinuous B-spline face refused by
name inside a hard time limit, at the default and a lowered cap; a smooth
B-spline dome still imports at its volume; degenerate knot variants end
cleanly) and step_guard's unit tests (division identical to truck's on a
smooth surface, bounded and tripped on a discontinuous one, no work left
running after a refused pass).

Fixes #1536

